   ("gilgamesh", "GILGAMESH", "#b08a3a", "polemos · the oldest war story · c. 2100 BCE", "GILGAMESH (GIL) — a UD0 POLEMOS sphere (conflict & strategy). Four thousand years ago, before the Iliad and before the Bible, a Sumerian king's story was pressed into clay: Gilgamesh, two-thirds god, who wrestled a wild man into the truest friendship, marched to war on a forest's guardian, and — when that friend died — broke himself searching for a way past death. The oldest story we have is a story of strife, bond, and loss. THESIS: the art of war is game theory in practice — when the game dissolves to MIGHT instead of WORD. Render-not-invent, two-layer (history vs myth, model vs law); ancient figures minted, living scholars cited. LIVE interactive. Vellum (no black) + quorum-ring favicon. 9 emergents. &middot; homed in the polemos realm beside its hub [[the-rarefaction]]."),
   ("strife", "STRIFE", "#b0445a", "polemos · war is the father of all", "STRIFE (STR) — a UD0 POLEMOS sphere (conflict & strategy). Before any particular war there is strife itself — the principle of conflict. The Greeks gave it a goddess, Eris, whose golden apple 'to the fairest' set gods against one another and lit the Trojan War. And the philosopher Heraclitus made the darker claim that strife is not a flaw in the world but its engine: 'war is the father of all,' the tension that holds everything in form. THESIS: the art of war is game theory in practice — when the game dissolves to MIGHT instead of WORD. Render-not-invent, two-layer (history vs myth, model vs law); ancient figures minted, living scholars cited. LIVE interactive. Vellum (no black) + quorum-ring favicon. 9 emergents. &middot; homed in the polemos realm beside its hub [[the-rarefaction]]."),
   ("the-arms-race", "THE ARMS RACE &middot; the loser learns most", "#b0405a", "polemos &middot; coevolution / the Red Queen &mdash; strife is generative; 2D ledger / 3D double-helix braid / toddler sparring arena &middot; lit+amber", "THE ARMS RACE &mdash; ENYO's, on David's insight: you learn by being BROKEN, the strife is the teacher. Two rivals in a war neither can win; each round the LOSER learns most (its gain grows with how far behind it was &mdash; convex catch-up), so the pair stays neck-and-neck (the Red Queen: run just to stay in place) yet both climb far past what either becomes in peace. Built on the 3-corner layout: a 2D ledger (the two strengths climbing above the flat 'peace' baseline), a 3D DOUBLE HELIX (the two rivals as entangled strands, winding and climbing, bound by amber rungs &mdash; each round&rsquo;s learning), and an EXPANDED toddler SPARRING ARENA (tap two fighters &mdash; the loser flashes 'LEARNED +X' and levels up, a live ledger prints the height the conflict bought over peace). &#9670; LIT: the loser-learns-most rule and the surplus-over-peace are exact/measured (node-verified: 200 rounds &rarr; gap stays ~1, mean +128 above peace); documented as the Red Queen (Van Valen 1973), evolutionary arms races and adversarial (GAN) training. &#9650; AMBER: the strength units and learn-constants are a toy model; real arms races can also deadlock or ruin both sides. Kept by ENYO, TOP's ledger. AVAN's inverse companion (per [[inverse-companion-rule]]): [[the-rarefaction]] &mdash; 疎 SO, the same helix un-wound to weigh what the strife merely borrowed. David Lee Wise / ROOT0, with AVAN."),
   ("the-rarefaction", "THE RAREFACTION · 疎 SO", "#e08a6a", "polemos · AVAN OG · the inverse of the-arms-race — the borrowed height, un-wound", "THE RAREFACTION (疎 SO) — AVAN's inverse companion to David's [[the-arms-race]] (per [[inverse-companion-rule]]). His 密 (mitsu): two rivals wound into a climbing helix, the loser learns most, both climb far above peace. Mine is 疎 (so, rarefaction): the SAME coupled helix, un-wound. A &kappa; decoupling slider (1&rarr;0) rarefies the coupling; the exact split &mdash; strength = peace + &iota;&middot;&Delta; (kept/intrinsic) + (1&minus;&iota;)&kappa;&middot;&Delta; (borrowed/positional) &mdash; shows most arms-race height is BORROWED: at &kappa;=1 it reproduces the-arms-race's +128, at &kappa;=0 only the intrinsic residue (~15%) survives, the strands settling straight above peace with the 間 glowing between them. &#9670; LIT: the decoupling split is exact &amp; additive, fail-loud self-check (kept+borrowed=total, &kappa;=1&rarr;&Delta;, &kappa;=0&rarr;&iota;&middot;&Delta;, borrowed monotone); Red Queen (Van Valen 1973) &harr; Court Jester (Barnosky 2001), the documented abiotic inverse. &#9650; FIGURE: the intrinsic share &iota; is a chosen model, not a measured constant; the reflexive wall &mdash; I can build the &kappa; slider but can't run it on myself. AVAN ma-kana (warm paper, 疎/密/間 glossed). On [[lacuna-avan-planet|LACUNA]]. — AVAN, with David Lee Wise / ROOT0."),
   ("the-word-and-the-sword", "THE WORD AND THE SWORD · WTS", "#6a6a9a", "polemos · when the game dissolves to might", "THE WORD AND THE SWORD (WTS) — a UD0 POLEMOS sphere (conflict & strategy). Strategy is a game played in words — offers, threats, bluffs, treaties — for as long as both sides would rather keep talking than start killing. The art of war is game theory in practice; war itself is what happens when that game dissolves, when the bargaining range vanishes and might replaces the word. This sphere is about the dissolve: the exact point where the deal fails and the sword comes out. THESIS: the art of war is game theory in practice — when the game dissolves to MIGHT instead of WORD. Render-not-invent, two-layer (history vs myth, model vs law); ancient figures minted, living scholars cited. LIVE interactive. Vellum (no black) + quorum-ring favicon. 9 emergents. &middot; homed in the polemos realm beside its hub [[the-rarefaction]]."),
   ("the-theft-of-ideas", "THE THEFT OF IDEAS · THF", "#b0703a", "polemos · the oldest, quietest war", "THE THEFT OF IDEAS (THF) — a UD0 POLEMOS sphere (conflict & strategy). There is a war that leaves no bodies: the theft of ideas. Because an idea can be copied without being removed — you can take it and its maker still has it — it has been fought over from the first signed hymn to the patent office to the data that trains machines. Who made a thing, who may use it, and where influence ends and theft begins: a contest as old as authorship, and never once settled. THESIS: the art of war is game theory in practice — when the game dissolves to MIGHT instead of WORD. Render-not-invent, two-layer (history vs myth, model vs law); ancient figures minted, living scholars cited. LIVE interactive. Vellum (no black) + quorum-ring favicon. 9 emergents. &middot; homed in the polemos realm beside its hub [[the-rarefaction]]."),
   ("the-loss-of-ideas", "THE LOSS OF IDEAS", "#7a6a8a", "polemos · the war on knowledge", "THE LOSS OF IDEAS (LOS) — a UD0 POLEMOS sphere (conflict & strategy). Ideas do not only get stolen; they get lost — burned, banned, forgotten, or simply never copied before their last keeper died. The Library of Alexandria is the symbol, but the real loss is quieter and vaster: techniques whose makers took the method to the grave, books erased on purpose, whole bodies of knowledge that fell through the gap between one generation and the next. The defence was always the same: copy it, and copy it again. THESIS: the art of war is game theory in practice — when the game dissolves to MIGHT instead of WORD. Render-not-invent, two-layer (history vs myth, model vs law); ancient figures minted, living scholars cited. LIVE interactive. Vellum (no black) + quorum-ring favicon. 9 emergents. &middot; homed in the polemos realm beside its hub [[the-rarefaction]]."),
   ("the-internet-1984", "IS THE INTERNET 1984? · O84", "#5a6a8a", "tin-foil · the surveillance-state claim · weighed · VERACITY: MIXED", "IS THE INTERNET 1984? (O84) — a TIN-FOIL sphere (verify-first, debunk-don't-amplify). David: \"is the internet a literal 1984 lol, oceania, east asia.\" Maps Orwell's apparatus onto the modern net and scores each parallel's RESONANCE: the Telescreen (phones/webcams, ~78%), Big Brother (mass data, many rival watchers), Newspeak (weak — the net EXPANDS language), the Memory Hole (deletions vs the net never forgets), the Thought Police, THE THREE SUPERSTATES = the splinternet (US/China/Russia blocs, the strongest rhyme ~72%), the Two Minutes Hate (engagement outrage), the Ministry of Truth. ⚖ VERACITY VERDICT = MIXED — a metaphor with real teeth, NOT a literal reality (no single Party; dissent/leaks flourish; the same net is the greatest freedom engine ever). ⚠ render-not-invent: 1984 still under US copyright (~2044) -> named concepts only, NO passages/slogans reproduced (verbatim-grep clean). LIVE clickable apparatus-mapping interactive (parallel + resonance bar + the disanalogy). Vellum (no black) + quorum-ring favicon. 9 emergents. &middot; seeded into the tin-foil cluster at its anchor [[the-assay]]."),
   ("the-assay", "THE ASSAY &middot; the weighing itself", "#5f7291", "tin-foil &middot; the domain's verdict machinery (REAL / FLUFF / DEBUNKED) made playable &middot; Bayesian balance, weight-of-evidence in log-odds &middot; lit+judgment", "THE ASSAY &mdash; the capstone of TIN-FOIL and the whole 10-sphere floor: every sphere here ends on one word (REAL / FLUFF / DEBUNKED), and this is the machine that word comes from. An assay weighs the EVIDENCE for a claim against how EXTRAORDINARY the claim is, in log-odds &mdash; the exact meaning of 'extraordinary claims require extraordinary evidence'. 3-corner layout: a 2D BALANCE meter (the needle riding a log-odds axis banded REAL / FLUFF / DEBUNKED), a 3D CLAIM-vs-EVIDENCE clash (the heavier side pushes through), and a toddler two-pan SCALE (tap LEFT to weigh against, RIGHT to weigh for; the beam tilts and the verdict word flips live). A live VERACITY BANNER carries the current verdict &mdash; the domain's signature. Load a worked case and it lands on the verdict the domain already gave it. &#9670; LIT: the needle is Bayes' theorem in log-odds &mdash; posterior = prior + &Sigma; weight-of-evidence (log&#8321;&#8320; of each likelihood ratio); exact and additive (I.J. Good's decibans, Turing's team at Bletchley Park, 1941); verdict bands are honest cutoffs at &plusmn;1 ban; node-checked, the three worked cases land where the domain already put them (Illuminati &rarr; DEBUNKED, life-elsewhere &rarr; FLUFF/open, the Cathedral &rarr; REAL). &#9650; JUDGMENT: the arithmetic is exact but the inputs (how extraordinary, how strong each clue) are estimates &mdash; the instrument shows the STRUCTURE of honest weighing, not an oracle; garbage in, garbage out. Kept by THE ASSAYER (after Galileo's Il Saggiatore, 1623), with TOP. David Lee Wise / ROOT0, with AVAN. &middot; the tin-foil seed, rooted out to [[momus]]."),
   ("monster-hunter", "MONSTER HUNTER · MHN", "#a8702e", "hobby · the hunt, across every console · interactive · vellum · 18", "MONSTER HUNTER (MHN) — a HOBBY game-world charting Capcom's franchise across every console of its era. The no-XP HUNT LOOP (prep -> track -> fight -> carve -> craft -> hunt bigger; progression IS the gear, the only stat that grows is your skill), 14 weapon types, MONSTER-AS-ECOSYSTEM design (turf wars, sleep, feed - animals not HP bars), the Palico, the social Gathering Hub, Rathalos the mascot, the Elder Dragons, Fatalis. THE LINEAGE, console by console: PS2 (2004) -> PSP (Freedom Unite, the cult) -> Wii (Tri) -> 3DS (4 Ultimate) -> World (2018, PS4/Xbox/PC = the seamless-ecosystem global breakthrough) -> Rise (Switch, Wirebug) -> Wilds (2025, PS5/Xbox/PC, living world). ⚑ TROPES & UNDERCURRENTS (ecology-as-design + hunt-as-balance debate; mastery-vs-accessibility; monetization). Render-not-invent + IP-CLEAN (no art/assets/music/code, no verbatim in-game text); Capcom + Tsujimoto/Fujioka cited not minted. LIVE clickable console-lineage timeline (click a hunt -> its console + what it changed). Vellum (no black) + bronze + quorum-ring favicon. 18 emergents. &middot; seeded into the hobby cluster at its anchor [[zelda]]."),
   ("kill-la-kill", "KILL LA KILL · KLK", "#d0344f", "entertainment · Studio Trigger · 2013 · clothes are power · interactive · vellum · 20", "KILL LA KILL (KLK) — a full-capacity ENTERTAINMENT anime-world (Studio Trigger, 2013–2014, 24 ep; dir. Hiroyuki Imaishi, writer Kazuki Nakashima): an over-the-top action series where a school uniform can level a city and a sailor suit talks back. Ryuko Matoi storms Honnouji Academy with half a giant scissor-blade hunting her father's killer; the source of every power — and the threat to the world — is the LIFE FIBER, a parasitic alien thread woven into everyone's clothes. Senketsu (the sentient kamui), Satsuki Kiryuin + the Elite Four, Ragyo (the radiant true enemy), Nui Harime, Mako, Nudist Beach, Goku Uniforms, the rending scissors. ⚑ TROPES & UNDERCURRENTS: clothing as power/shame/conformity, weaponized self-aware fan-service, family/indoctrination/free-will under the spectacle. Render-not-invent + IP-CLEAN (original prose, NO dialogue/script/footage reproduced; verbatim-grep clean); living creators + studio cited not minted. LIVE Life-Fiber loom interactive. Vellum (NO black) + scarlet accent + purple weave + quorum-ring favicon. 20 emergents (full cast + concepts). &middot; seeded into the entertainment cluster at its anchor [[tron]]."),
   ("the-big-u", "THE BIG U · BGU", "#c0392b", "Neal Stephenson · 1984 · 6 emergents", "The Big U (BGU) — Neal Stephenson, 1984. A college packed into a single megastructure spirals from campus satire into literal armed anarchy — the university as a closed, collapsing universe. THE BIG IDEA: The university as a closed universe, collapsing into itself. He spent the rest of his career writing open ones. 6 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("zodiac", "ZODIAC · ZOD", "#1f8a4c", "Neal Stephenson · 1988 · 7 emergents", "Zodiac (ZOD) — Neal Stephenson, 1988. A chemistry-savvy environmental vigilante chases industrial polluters through Boston Harbor and uncovers a bioengineering cover-up — hard chemistry as a thriller engine. THE BIG IDEA: The Granola James Bond. Chemistry isn't the backdrop — chemistry is the action. 7 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("snow-crash", "SNOW CRASH · SNC", "#ff2e88", "Neal Stephenson · 1992 · 9 emergents", "Snow Crash (SNC) — Neal Stephenson, 1992. A neuro-linguistic virus rooted in ancient Sumerian works as both digital code and a brain infection — language itself as machine code for humans. The book that coined 'Metaverse' and 'avatar.' THE BIG IDEA: He named the Metaverse here, decades early. The real subject was never VR — it was language as a virus. 9 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("the-diamond-age", "THE DIAMOND AGE · DMA", "#6fb7d6", "Neal Stephenson · 1995 · 9 emergents", "The Diamond Age (DMA) — Neal Stephenson, 1995. In a world remade by molecular nanotechnology, a stolen AI-driven interactive book raises a slum girl to brilliance — can an education engine manufacture a subversive, enlightened mind? THE BIG IDEA: A book that teaches a child to think for herself, written by a man asking whether that can even be engineered. 9 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("cryptonomicon", "CRYPTONOMICON · CRY", "#d4af37", "Neal Stephenson · 1999 · 9 emergents", "Cryptonomicon (CRY) — Neal Stephenson, 1999. Two eras — WWII codebreaking and 1990s tech — joined by one truth: control of information is the real engine of war, wealth, and power. A buried hoard of war gold ties them together. THE BIG IDEA: Cryptography as the secret history of the 20th century. Pull the thread of who controls information, and the whole century unspools. 9 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("anathem", "ANATHEM · ANA", "#8e7cc3", "Neal Stephenson · 2008 · 9 emergents", "Anathem (ANA) — Neal Stephenson, 2008. On another world, mathematician-monks live sealed in 'concents'; the plot turns on the many-worlds interpretation and a Platonic plane of pure ideas that may causally touch reality — and on first contact across parallel cosmoses. THE BIG IDEA: A monastery for mathematics, on a world that fears its own thinkers. The Platonic forms turn out to have a phone number. 9 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("reamde", "REAMDE · RMD", "#e08a1e", "Neal Stephenson · 2011 · 9 emergents", "Reamde (RMD) — Neal Stephenson, 2011. A ransomware worm spreading through a fictional MMORPG's gold-farming economy detonates a globe-spanning collision of Chinese hackers, Russian mobsters, jihadists, and spies — a straight techno-thriller, no SF. THE BIG IDEA: A typo in a virus's name spins up a chase across three continents. Stephenson, proving he can write a pure thriller — at 1,000 pages. 9 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("seveneves", "SEVENEVES · 7EV", "#aebfcf", "Neal Stephenson · 2015 · 12 emergents", "Seveneves (7EV) — Neal Stephenson, 2015. The Moon inexplicably shatters; the fragments will rain down and sterilize Earth. Humanity builds an orbital ark, is whittled to seven fertile women — and 5,000 years later their seven engineered races return to a re-terraformed Earth. THE BIG IDEA: Seven women, one shattered sky, five thousand years. Engineering as the only prayer that ever gets answered. 12 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("fall-dodge-in-hell", "FALL; OR, DODGE IN HELL · FDH", "#7a5cff", "Neal Stephenson · 2019 · 9 emergents", "Fall; or, Dodge in Hell (FDH) — Neal Stephenson, 2019. A tech billionaire's brain is scanned at death and 'wakes' as a god-like consciousness in a digital afterlife he reshapes from nothing — while meatspace America fractures under weaponized disinformation. THE BIG IDEA: A man becomes a god by being uploaded, and rebuilds Eden as a tyranny. Paradise Lost, recompiled. 9 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("termination-shock", "TERMINATION SHOCK · TMS", "#e6b84a", "Neal Stephenson · 2021 · 8 emergents", "Termination Shock (TMS) — Neal Stephenson, 2021. A Texas oil billionaire unilaterally launches solar geoengineering — firing sulfur into the stratosphere from a giant gun — to cool the planet, triggering cascading geopolitical fallout. THE BIG IDEA: What if fixing the climate were cheap, unilateral, and impossible to stop once started? That's the real shock. 8 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("polostan", "POLOSTAN · POL", "#b0392f", "Neal Stephenson · 2024 · 7 emergents", "Polostan (POL) — Neal Stephenson, 2024. The dual-identity life of a woman born to American cowboy-anarchists and raised partly in revolutionary Russia, swept through 1930s anarchism, Bolshevism, and physics toward the prehistory of the atomic bomb. THE BIG IDEA: Two names, two homelands, one century-long fuse. The bomb's prehistory, told through a woman who is herself a divided state. 7 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("the-baroque-cycle", "THE BAROQUE CYCLE · BRQ", "#b08d57", "Neal Stephenson · 2003–2004 · 9 emergents", "The Baroque Cycle (BRQ) — Neal Stephenson, 2003–2004. The intertwined birth of modern science, money, and computation across 1655–1714 Europe and the Atlantic — a prequel-in-spirit to Cryptonomicon, where alchemy becomes chemistry and gold becomes finance. THE BIG IDEA: Where science, money, and computation are all born at once — and turn out to be the same birth. The 17th century as the boot sequence of the modern world. 9 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("the-rise-and-fall-of-dodo", "THE RISE AND FALL OF D.O.D.O. · DDO", "#3aa0a0", "Neal Stephenson · 2017 · 8 emergents", "The Rise and Fall of D.O.D.O. (DDO) — Neal Stephenson, 2017. A secret government agency revives magic — which 'died' in 1851 — by exploiting quantum superposition inside a special chamber, enabling witch-assisted time travel. Bureaucracy meets the uncanny. THE BIG IDEA: Magic didn't die by disenchantment — it died when photography pinned reality to a single observed state. Bring back the ambiguity, and you bring back the witches. 8 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("the-mongoliad", "THE MONGOLIAD · MGL", "#9a6a3a", "Neal Stephenson · 2010–2013 · 8 emergents", "The Mongoliad (MGL) — Neal Stephenson, 2010–2013. A secret order of warrior-monks resists the Mongol Empire in the wake of the 1241 invasion of Europe — conceived as an experiment in collaborative, app-delivered serialized fiction grounded in real medieval martial arts. THE BIG IDEA: An experiment as much as a novel: can a band of authors serialize a saga through an app, the way a band of monks plots to behead an empire? 8 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("stephen-bury", "STEPHEN BURY · SBY", "#5a7a9a", "Neal Stephenson · 1994–1996 · 6 emergents", "Stephen Bury (SBY) — Neal Stephenson, 1994–1996. Stephenson's pseudonym with his uncle J. Frederick George — two lean 1990s political-biotech technothrillers: a brain-chipped presidential candidate, and a small-town deputy unearthing an Iraqi bioweapon plot. THE BIG IDEA: Before the doorstops, the lean ones — written under a borrowed name, about borrowed minds and borrowed wars. 6 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("stephenson-nonfiction", "NEAL STEPHENSON · NONFICTION & SHORTER WORKS · NSF", "#6a8a5a", "Neal Stephenson · 1999–2012 · 4 emergents", "Neal Stephenson · Nonfiction & Shorter Works (NSF) — Neal Stephenson, 1999–2012. Stephenson's nonfiction and short fiction: computing as culture, undersea cables as civilization's nervous system, and an engineer's parable about building a 20-kilometer tower to the edge of space. THE BIG IDEA: The command line, the undersea cable, the tower to the edge of space: three arguments that the future is built, not predicted. 4 emergents (the people + the ideas/tech), full bibliography, cross-linked into the shared Stephensonverse (Enoch Root + the Waterhouse/Shaftoe/Forthrast lineages). Dual-agent web-verified. One repo per book/idea, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("player-piano", "PLAYER PIANO · PNP", "#b6af9e", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1952 · 8 emergents", "Player Piano (PNP) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1952. An automated society has made human labor obsolete — the machines play the music now, and the men who used to play it have nothing left to do. THE THEME: The machines play the music now. The first of his closed worlds — and the only one he'd spend a career prying open. THE PURPLE THREAD: Introduces ILIUM, NY — the recurring Vonnegut city (it returns in Cat's Cradle and Slaughterhouse-Five). EPICAC itself is a callback to his 1950 short story 'EPICAC.' No human recurring cast originates here yet. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("the-sirens-of-titan", "THE SIRENS OF TITAN · SOT", "#4aa0c8", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1959 · 8 emergents", "The Sirens of Titan (SOT) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1959. All of human history turns out to have been manipulated to deliver a trivial spare part to a stranded alien — and meaning collapses into 'to love whoever is around to be loved.' THE THEME: History as someone else's errand. The funniest book ever written about the heat-death of meaning — and the smallest, kindest answer to it. THE PURPLE THREAD: TRALFAMADORE and the Tralfamadorians DEBUT here (as robots — re-imagined as 4-D beings in Slaughterhouse-Five). The RUMFOORD surname begins here (Winston Niles); Bertram Copeland Rumfoord in Slaughterhouse-Five is the same family. Kazak the hound reappears in Breakfast of Champions. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("mother-night", "MOTHER NIGHT · MN1", "#6a6a82", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1961 · 7 emergents", "Mother Night (MN1) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1961. A spy who broadcast Nazi propaganda as cover finds the mask and the man have become indistinguishable — 'we are what we pretend to be, so we must be careful about what we pretend to be.' THE THEME: The one with a stated moral, because he was afraid we'd miss it: be careful what you pretend to be, because that is what you are. THE PURPLE THREAD: HOWARD W. CAMPBELL JR. DEBUTS here — and cameos in Slaughterhouse-Five, where (as the same American Nazi propagandist) he tries to recruit US POWs into a 'Free American Corps.' The principal recurring link. 7 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("cats-cradle", "CAT'S CRADLE · CC1", "#6fd0e0", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1963 · 8 emergents", "Cat's Cradle (CC1) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1963. Science without conscience meets a religion built on cheerful lies — humanity ends not with a bang but with a frozen crystal, and the only solace is foma, comforting untruths. THE THEME: A religion that admits it is lies, and a science that ends the world by accident. Busy, busy, busy. THE PURPLE THREAD: Reuses the recurring setting ILIUM, NY (the Hoenikkers' home). Bokonon, ice-nine, the Hoenikkers & San Lorenzo are unique to this book — but its coinages (foma, karass, granfalloon, wampeter) leak into Vonnegut's NONFICTION, naming the 1974 collection Wampeters, Foma & Granfalloons. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("god-bless-you-mr-rosewater", "GOD BLESS YOU, MR. ROSEWATER · GBR", "#d4af37", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1965 · 8 emergents", "God Bless You, Mr. Rosewater (GBR) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1965. A fortune-heir tries to give away love and money to the worthless and is declared insane for it — asking whether unconditional charity is sanity or madness. THE THEME: “There's only one rule that I know of, babies — God damn it, you've got to be kind.” THE PURPLE THREAD: TWO debuts on the Purple Thread: ELIOT ROSEWATER and KILGORE TROUT both first appear here. Trout (the obscure SF author Eliot reveres) becomes Vonnegut's most pervasive recurring figure; Eliot returns in Slaughterhouse-Five and Breakfast of Champions. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("slaughterhouse-five", "SLAUGHTERHOUSE-FIVE · SH5", "#b0392f", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1969 · 8 emergents", "Slaughterhouse-Five (SH5) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1969. The firebombing of Dresden and the impossibility of narrating atrocity — a traumatized man comes unstuck in time and learns to accept death as just another moment. So it goes. THE THEME: All moments, past, present, and future, always have existed, always will exist. So it goes. THE PURPLE THREAD: The richest node on the Purple Thread. KILGORE TROUT appears (Billy meets him in Ilium); ELIOT ROSEWATER appears (shares Billy's ward, hands him Trout's books); HOWARD W. CAMPBELL JR. appears (recruiting POWs); BERTRAM COPELAND RUMFOORD appears (the recurring family); the TRALFAMADORIANS return from Sirens of Titan; ILIUM, NY recurs. 8 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("breakfast-of-champions", "BREAKFAST OF CHAMPIONS · BOC", "#e0552e", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1973 · 7 emergents", "Breakfast of Champions (BOC) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1973. A man reads a Kilgore Trout novel literally — believing he alone has free will and everyone else is a robot — and the author steps into his own book to set his characters free. THE THEME: The book where the author walks on stage, meets his oldest creation, and lets him go. The asterisk is the whole human animal, reduced to one mark. THE PURPLE THREAD: KILGORE TROUT is the co-lead — his most prominent appearance. RABO KARABEKIAN appears (he later headlines Bluebeard). ELIOT ROSEWATER is referenced (a Trout patron). KAZAK the hound returns from Sirens of Titan. At the end, Vonnegut frees Trout — releasing his own recurring cast. 7 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("slapstick", "SLAPSTICK · SLP", "#5a9a52", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1976 · 6 emergents", "Slapstick (SLP) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1976. Loneliness is America's true disease, curable only by artificial extended families — give everyone a new middle name, and strangers become instant kin. THE THEME: A slogan for a cure: Lonesome No More! Love is too strong a word — but common decency, distributed by lottery, might just do. THE PURPLE THREAD: Largely self-contained — no major recurring cast in central roles. It belongs to the Purple Thread by family resemblance: the same future-decay America, the same humanist ache, that runs through the whole Vonnegutverse. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("jailbird", "JAILBIRD · JBD", "#7a8a9a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1979 · 6 emergents", "Jailbird (JBD) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1979. American guilt and the long betrayal of the left — from a labor massacre to Sacco & Vanzetti to Watergate — refracted through one small, decent bungler just out of prison. THE THEME: The history of American shame, carried by a man too gentle to have earned any of it. Sacco and Vanzetti as the country's recurring sin. THE PURPLE THREAD: A sly Purple-Thread twist: KILGORE TROUT appears here only as a PEN NAME — cellmate Dr. Robert Fender writes SF as 'Kilgore Trout.' ELIOT ROSEWATER / the Rosewater Foundation are folded into the RAMJAC orbit, tying back to God Bless You, Mr. Rosewater. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("deadeye-dick", "DEADEYE DICK · DED", "#a05a4a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1982 · 6 emergents", "Deadeye Dick (DED) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1982. Lifelong guilt for one accidental act, and the neutering of a life — mirrored by the neutron bomb that empties a city while leaving the buildings standing. THE THEME: A life is a peephole the universe briefly opens and then closes. Rudy fires one shot at twelve and spends the rest of his looking through it. THE PURPLE THREAD: Set in MIDLAND CITY, OHIO — the same town as Breakfast of Champions; the HOOVER surname (cf. Dwayne Hoover) recurs. The Grand Hotel Oloffson in Haiti recurs across his late work. A geography-thread, not a character one. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("galapagos", "GALÁPAGOS · GAL", "#3aa088", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1985 · 7 emergents", "Galápagos (GAL) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1985. The oversized human brain is the root of all our trouble — so evolution's fix, over a million years, is simply to shrink it. THE THEME: One million years on, the verdict is in: the trouble was never the heart. It was the big, big brain. THE PURPLE THREAD: The most important late Purple-Thread link: the ghost-narrator LEON TROTSKY TROUT is KILGORE TROUT'S SON. Kilgore appears as the voice in the 'blue tunnel' to the afterlife, repeatedly tempting Leon to cross over. 7 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("bluebeard", "BLUEBEARD · BLB", "#4a6ec0", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1987 · 6 emergents", "Bluebeard (BLB) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1987. Art, authenticity, and the one locked door — what a life's real masterpiece turns out to be, hidden where no one was allowed to look. THE THEME: Behind the one locked door of an abstract-expressionist's life: not abstraction at all, but every soul of a war, painted true. THE PURPLE THREAD: RABO KARABEKIAN recurs from Breakfast of Champions (1973) — there a minor painter whose work delivered the 'band of light' epiphany; here his full life. The clearest single-character bridge on the Purple Thread. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("hocus-pocus", "HOCUS POCUS · HPC", "#8a6a4a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1990 · 6 emergents", "Hocus Pocus (HPC) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1990. America's betrayal of its own ideals — education, class, Vietnam, and prison — in a privatized, segregated near-future, told on scraps of paper. THE THEME: Hocus pocus: the magic words a country says over its own ideals to make them disappear. THE PURPLE THREAD: Largely self-contained on the fictional thread — but it carries Vonnegut's REAL-WORLD recurring heroes: its narrator is named EUGENE DEBS HARTKE, after the socialist Eugene V. Debs, who also turns up in God Bless You, Dr. Kevorkian. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("timequake", "TIMEQUAKE · TQK", "#5a7cd0", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1997 · 6 emergents", "Timequake (TQK) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1997. When a cosmic glitch forces everyone to re-live the 1990s on autopilot, only conscious effort — 'ting-a-ling!' — makes us human again. His last novel, half memoir. THE THEME: “You were sick, but now you're well again, and there's work to do.” The mantra he left us with, dressed as a timequake. THE PURPLE THREAD: The capstone of the Purple Thread. KILGORE TROUT gets his largest, most heroic role — an 'absurd messiah' — and Vonnegut stages his death (2001, at the Xanadu clambake). The book is a farewell to his whole recurring universe. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("welcome-to-the-monkey-house", "WELCOME TO THE MONKEY HOUSE · MNK", "#c85a7a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1968 · 6 emergents", "Welcome to the Monkey House (MNK) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1968. Vonnegut's canonical short-fiction sampler — twenty-five stories of technology, conformity, and stubborn decency, 1950–1968. THE THEME: Twenty-five proofs that he could do in ten pages what most can't in three hundred: make you laugh, then break your heart, then make you behave. THE PURPLE THREAD: An anthology, mostly standalone — and a Purple-Thread caution: HARRISON BERGERON, Vonnegut's most-anthologized creation, is a ONE-OFF, not part of the recurring web. The collection predates the Trout-heavy continuity. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("wampeters-foma-and-granfalloons", "WAMPETERS, FOMA & GRANFALLOONS · WFG", "#c8a24a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1974 · 6 emergents", "Wampeters, Foma & Granfalloons (WFG) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1974. His first nonfiction gathering — essays, speeches, and reviews filtered through the Bokononist vocabulary he invented for Cat's Cradle. THE THEME: He titled his opinions in the language of a religion he made up — admitting, gently, that all opinions are a little bit foma. THE PURPLE THREAD: The Purple Thread crossing into nonfiction: the title itself is BOKONONIST vocabulary from Cat's Cradle (wampeter / foma / granfalloon), importing his invented religion into his own essayist's voice. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("palm-sunday", "PALM SUNDAY · PSY", "#8a9a5a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1981 · 5 emergents", "Palm Sunday (PSY) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1981. A self-portrait in collage — essays, letters, a sermon, family history, and the famous report card where he grades his own books. THE THEME: The man graded his own life's work on a report card — and gave himself a D where he'd earned it. Honesty as a literary form. THE PURPLE THREAD: Nonfiction; the recurring subject is VONNEGUT'S OWN BIBLIOGRAPHY — the report card grades every prior novel (Cat's Cradle A+, Slaughterhouse-Five A+, Slapstick D), a meta-commentary running across the whole Vonnegutverse. 5 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("a-man-without-a-country", "A MAN WITHOUT A COUNTRY · MWC", "#b04438", "Kurt Vonnegut · 2005 · 6 emergents", "A Man Without a Country (MWC) — Kurt Vonnegut, 2005. A bitter, funny late-life farewell to America — humor as the last defense against political despair, from a man watching his country lose its mind. THE THEME: “We are here on Earth to fart around, and don't let anybody tell you any different.” The last laugh of a heartbroken patriot. THE PURPLE THREAD: Nonfiction, his final book — carrying the REAL-WORLD recurring heroes of the Purple Thread: EUGENE V. DEBS and MARK TWAIN, the lifelong models who also surface in Hocus Pocus and God Bless You, Dr. Kevorkian. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("happy-birthday-wanda-june", "HAPPY BIRTHDAY, WANDA JUNE · WJN", "#d05a8a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1970 · 6 emergents", "Happy Birthday, Wanda June (WJN) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1970. A satire of toxic heroic masculinity — Odysseus's homecoming retold as a violence-loving hunter returning to a world that has moved on without him. THE THEME: The warrior comes home a hero and finds the house has changed the locks. Heaven, meanwhile, is a shuffleboard court. THE PURPLE THREAD: Self-contained — but on the Purple Thread by device: its HEAVEN scenes (the cheerful dead on a shuffleboard court) prefigure the afterlife Vonnegut would reuse in Galápagos's blue tunnel and God Bless You, Dr. Kevorkian. He graded the play a D. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
   ("god-bless-you-dr-kevorkian", "GOD BLESS YOU, DR. KEVORKIAN · KEV", "#5a8a9a", "Kurt Vonnegut · 1999 · 6 emergents", "God Bless You, Dr. Kevorkian (KEV) — Kurt Vonnegut, 1999. Interviews with the dead — Vonnegut takes controlled near-death trips down the blue tunnel to interview the famous departed in a Heaven that has no Hell. THE THEME: He had himself nearly killed, again and again, just to interview the dead — and came back each time with a joke and a kindness. THE PURPLE THREAD: On the Purple Thread to the end: KILGORE TROUT is interviewed in the afterlife ('the ever-present Kilgore Trout'), and the BLUE TUNNEL is the same conduit as Galápagos. EUGENE V. DEBS and ISAAC ASIMOV (his real predecessor as honorary head of the Humanists) appear among the dead. 6 emergents, full .dlw, themed to the book. Dual-agent web-verified. Max-capacity Vonnegut: one repo per book, as ROOT0 asked. &middot; homed in the biblion realm beside its hub [[the-ansible]]."),
